if (r && r.status === 200) { serveHit(res, {...}, r.buf, 'HIT-COALESCED', seq); return; }
```

The in-flight promise is resolved with the upstream's raw `ur.statusCode` regardless of cacheability:

```js
// proxy-a.mjs:319 (resolve after upstream ends)
resolve({ status: ur.statusCode, contentType, buf, model, usage: u });
```

Cacheability itself is a separate check made only by the *first* (leader) request when deciding whether to persist to disk:

```js
// proxy-a.mjs:294 (only complete 200s get written to the cache)
const complete = ur.statusCode === 200 && (!wantsStream || text.includes('message_stop'));
if (complete && !bypass) { /* write .bin/.json */ }
```

So if the upstream returns HTTP 200 but the result isn't cacheable (e.g. an incomplete/truncated stream missing `message_stop`, per `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s "Only complete 200s cached" rule), `r.status` is still `200`. The waiter goes through the `r.status === 200` branch and is served via `serveHit(..., 'HIT-COALESCED', ...)`, which writes `x-cache: HIT-COALESCED` — even though nothing was actually written to disk for that response. Only a genuinely non-200 upstream result produces `x-cache: MISS-COALESCED` instead.
